From 3db804745c0b2c7cafa5d4b0aa67d5fcd76dac04 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 12:27:53 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Windows Service hosting & recovery: fix name mismatch, add
 automatic recovery, add full control script set

Last item on the original roadmap - everything else (foundation, print-
flow migration, legacy cleanup, outbox/Hangfire, reconciliation
credential) was already delivered. This is scripts and documentation
only; no C# changed.

Two real, confirmed bugs fixed in the scripts actually used today
(publish/install-service.bat, publish/uninstall-service.bat), not
assumed - read directly before editing:

1. Service name mismatch. SERVICE_NAME was "MyWindowsServiceApp", but
   Program.cs's AddWindowsService uses ServiceName "InvetoryServices" -
   the SCM-registered name and the running process's own internal
   identity have never matched. Fixed to "InvetoryServices" on both
   scripts, matching the app's actual identity rather than an
   unrelated placeholder string.

2. No automatic-recovery configuration at all. The previous
   install-service.bat called only `sc create ... start= auto` - no
   `sc failure`. A crash simply left the service down until someone
   noticed; there was nothing to observe or verify, because nothing
   was configured. Added: restart 5 seconds after each of the first
   three unexpected exits within a rolling 24-hour window, then stop
   trying - a persistently crashing service needs a human, not an
   infinite restart loop that hides the underlying problem.

Also added to both the fixed publish/ scripts and a new canonical
deploy/ folder: start-service.bat, stop-service.bat, restart-service.bat
(stops, waits for a confirmed STOPPED state via polling rather than a
fixed sleep, then starts - avoids racing a slow shutdown), and
status-service.bat (read-only, shows both current state and the
configured recovery actions - the only one of the six that doesn't
need elevation, useful for verifying install-service.bat's recovery
configuration actually took effect without needing admin rights just
to check).

New deploy/ folder is the canonical, maintained copy going forward -
confirmed via the tracked FolderProfile.pubxml that the actual
configured dotnet publish output target is
bin\Release\net8.0\publish\ inside the project folder, which is a
different location from the repo-root publish\ folder these scripts
have historically lived in. That means the repo-root publish\ folder
is not something `dotnet publish` will ever regenerate or keep in
sync - scripts living only there were one accidental deletion away
from being lost with no build step to recreate them. Fixed the
existing copies in place (since that's what's actually being run
today) and added the canonical set in deploy/, which isn't inside any
build-output path at all.

deploy/README.md: complete deployment checklist - every appsettings.json
key and every secret this service now needs across all the phases
delivered this series (Cors, MachineCommunication, Outbox, InventoryApi,
PrintAgentAuth, ReconciliationCredential), which secret goes where
(user-secrets for dev, environment variable with the double-underscore
convention for production - user-secrets don't exist on a production
machine at all), first-time install steps, and an end-to-end
verification sequence including deliberately killing the process to
confirm automatic recovery actually works rather than just trusting
that sc failure was configured correctly.

One thing found but deliberately not fixed as part of this phase,
documented instead: this repo has bin/, obj/, and .vs/ in .gitignore,
but a large number of files under
invetoryBackGroundServices/bin/Release/net8.0/publish/ and the
repo-root publish/ folder - including compiled DLLs, the .exe, and
actual runtime log files under publish/AppLog/ - are already tracked
in git history from before .gitignore existed (an ignore rule never
retroactively untracks a file). Confirmed by grep against git ls-files
before writing this note, not assumed. This is a real repo-hygiene
issue and the AppLog contents may be worth treating as sensitive, but
untracking + rewriting history is a bigger, more disruptive operation
than adding install scripts and deserves its own explicit decision
rather than being bundled in here silently.

No dotnet SDK involved in this phase at all - these are batch scripts
and documentation, not C#. Verified by: every goto label referenced
actually exists as a target in the same file; parenthesis balance
across every script (batch's if/else blocks are paren-delimited, same
class of check as brace-balance on the C# phases); and a plain read-
through of the final commands themselves for correctness (sc syntax,
the STOPPED-polling loop logic in uninstall/restart), since there's no
compiler equivalent for batch scripts to lean on the way there was for
every prior phase.
